ideas.profile_evidence:
Stdlib counters (frozen bin 4587ce9e1a23, all flags on vs all minus solver; research/ideas/redundancy/c4/empty__*.txt):
- solver.probes=338, solver.prunedChoices=0, and no solver.commits;
- solver.forks=316, the same as the baseline;
- solver.goalSteps 9484 -> 10622 (+12%);
- solver.refreshScans 63 -> 3052 (48x). Stale goals stay in the stale list while overloads are parked, and every assume() re-reifies all of them.
So on the stdlib `solver` is pure overhead. In allon.txt, Solver.probe is 2.81% inclusive and resolveParked 5.37%. In interleaved stdlib timings (load about 9, noisy; research/ideas/redundancy/timing-stdlib.txt), all-minus-solver was faster than all-on in 3/3 rounds (433/477, 401/472, 354/389 ms). probes - forks = 22, so only about 11 resolveParked calls ever had more than one parked set. overload-priority.hylo: baseline overloads=3 in 9 systems (one overload per system). With solver: commits=2, prunedChoices=2. The divergence (node 0:75 typed X vs A<Void>) comes from a commit in a single-overload system. ovl-8-8 (64 probes, 8 commits) and ovr-3-10 (30 probes, 10 commits) have 8 and 10 simultaneous overload sets, so the gate keeps parking there.

measurements:
All measurements are counters from the frozen binary research/exp/bin/4587ce9e1a23/tc-bench with HYLO_TC_ABSDEPTH=2. Each workload was run with the baseline, all six flags, each leave-one-out set, and each flag alone. The stdlib matrix was re-run today into /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/c4. The stress-workload matrix is the earlier run in /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/c2: its stdlib all-on counters match today's exactly, so the counters are deterministic and the code is the same. The script is /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/matrix3.sh. The tables, produced by /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/scripts/loo.py, are in /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/loo-tables.txt.

How much work each flag still removes when the other five are on (all on vs all minus that flag):

- names. It is the most important flag on the stdlib:
  - stdlib: subgoals 646 -> 291, matches 14079 -> 3541, layers 5473 -> 227, applies calls 7216 -> 32, index.unavailable 1367 -> 888, absmodel.refuted 225 -> 104;
  - members-big: matches 3,968,000 -> 0;
  - ovl-8-8: matches 144 -> 112;
  - ovr-3-10: matches 32 -> 20.
  Its savings are summon work inside the slow path of applies(), the where-clause matching of conditional extensions; scanning fewer extensions is a small part. It is not redundant, but it is unsound (review), and memberindex would subsume it.
- index:
  - stdlib: matches 11180 -> 3541;
  - breadth80: 517680 -> 6480;
  - chain8: 649 -> 19;
  - members-big, div-*, ovl and ovr: zero extra effect (absmodel already cuts the same threads).
- absmodel:
  - stdlib: subgoals 327 -> 291, matches 4057 -> 3541, layers 341 -> 227;
  - div-grow-20: 211 subgoals / 400 matches -> 21 / 20 (essential there);
  - div-dead-20: 63 / 104 -> 21 / 20;
  - members-big: 1200 -> 0 matches;
  - breadth80 and chain: 0 refutations, so pure cost. On breadth80, absmodel.micros is 35-53 ms of about 350-430 ms user typing. Each user module also rebuilds a 63-65 fact model costing 1.4-2.2 ms (chain8 about 50% of user typing).
- solver:
  - ovl-8-8: matches 50617 -> 112, goalSteps 384715 -> 435;
  - ovr-3-10: 75782 -> 20;
  - stdlib: 0 pruned, 0 commits, 338 probes, goalSteps 9484 -> 10622, refreshScans 63 -> 3052. So on the stdlib it is overhead.
- loopcheck: loopcheck.pruned=0 and all counters are identical to all-on on every workload. It prunes only when absmodel is off (div-dead-20 and div-loop-20: 21).
- unify: it changes no counter on any workload. Its effect is constant-factor only and cannot be assessed without timing or perf. I suggest adding a counter of dynamic casts in unifiable.

Interactions:
- index and absmodel are complementary. index is needed for the stdlib, breadth80 and chain; absmodel for div-grow. Either one suffices for members-big, ovl and ovr.
- names and index add up; they do not overlap.
- loopcheck is subsumed by absmodel on all workloads, but not on the review programs: lc08 and lc14 exhaust memory or produce no output in the baseline and under all minus loopcheck, while loopcheck terminates.

Loopcheck soundness, re-run today on the review programs (/home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/lc, dumps normalized as in tcx.py):
- lc04, lc05, lc06, lc07 and lc12 differ from the baseline with loopcheck alone and with all six flags (loopcheck.pruned=1-2).
- All flags minus loopcheck is identical to the baseline on all of those, and on lc01-03, lc10b, lc11b, lc13 and lc15.
- lc09 gave no output in any configuration.

Configuration hazard (index,absmodel on the stdlib):
- without HYLO_TC_ABSDEPTH (k=3): absmodel.work=3,768,008, absmodel.micros 14.0 s, type.stdlib 14876 ms, refuted=224;
- with k=2: work=14783, micros 12.5 ms, type.stdlib 796 ms, refuted=225.

Timing, which is very noisy (load average 5-10 on 4 cores; /home/user/hylo-new/research/ideas/redundancy/timing-stdlib.txt, min of 5 repeats, 3 interleaved rounds):
- all minus solver was faster than all-on in 3/3 rounds (-7 to -15%);
- the leave-one-out runs for unify, loopcheck and absmodel were inconclusive.

Recommended simplification:
- drop loopcheck: it is redundant and unsound; replace it with loopsafe if the termination niche matters;
- replace solver with solver1;
- keep index and absmodel, with abslite and absseed;
- retire names once memberindex lands; both still need the review's taint fix to be sound;
- dtree (from the earlier lens) would supersede index.
